 "proposed_edges": [
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_01",
   "source": "sciencewars",
   "target": "ssk",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Scientists and philosophers charge that the strong programme and The Golem leave nature out of the explanation of scientific belief (1994–98)",
   "map_label": "Strong programme and The Golem attacked, 1994–98",
   "evidence_note": "In-period texts read. Sokal, \"What the Social Text Affair Does and Does Not Prove\" (author's text, 1997; printed 1998; cited by note): \"The extreme versions of social constructivism and relativism -- such as the Edinburgh 'strong programme' -- are, I think, largely based on the same failure to distinguish clearly between ontology, epistemology, and the sociology of knowledge\"; he quotes Collins (n. 10) as saying \"the natural world has a small or non-existent role in the construction of scientific knowledge\", while allowing that Collins may intend a methodological injunction. Sokal and Bricmont, Intellectual Impostures (1998; Profile 1999/2003 printing): most scientists \"point out the substantial missing piece in this kind of explanation: Nature itself\" (p. 79); the strong programme \"is ambiguous in its intent; and, depending on how one resolves the ambiguity, it becomes either a valid and mildly interesting corrective to the most naive psychological and sociological notions - reminding us that 'true beliefs have causes too' - or else a gross and blatant error\" (p. 84). Mermin, Physics Today 49:3 (1996), p. 11: Collins and Pinch \"infer from these studies a seriously deficient picture of the scientific enterprise\"; The Golem is \"an instructive demonstration of what can go wrong if you focus too strongly on the social perspective\". Mermin frames this as a question of balance, not a refutation. Gross and Levitt (1994; book unread) are known here only through Edge (1997, n. 4), who reports that they name \"the 'strong programme' in sociology of science associated with the Edinburgh school\" (p. 25) and Bloor (p. 52) while listing no work by Barnes or Bloor in their references. The replies are the separate ssk → sciencewars edge.",
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_sokal_affair_1998",
    "ss136_sokal_bricmont_1998",
    "ss136_mermin_1996",
    "ss136_edge_1997",
    "ss136_gross_levitt_1994"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": "ssk/strong_programme",
   "target_strand_note": "strong_programme holds Bloor, Barnes and Henry, whose programme Sokal, Sokal and Bricmont and (via Edge) Gross and Levitt name. Mermin's target, The Golem, belongs with ssk/replication (Collins and Pinch); one arrow carries both.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_02",
   "source": "ssk",
   "target": "sciencewars",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Sociologists of scientific knowledge answer that their methodological relativism was misread as hostility to science (1996–97)",
   "map_label": "SSK replies: \"scientific fundamentalists\", 1996–97",
   "evidence_note": "In-period replies in the sociologists' own words. Collins and Pinch, letter in Physics Today 49:7 (1996), p. 11, answering Mermin: \"Many scientists, especially those who have become spokespersons, are evidently scientific fundamentalists. ... Where we sociologists think of ourselves as trying to gain social and historical insight into science, the fundamentalists see heresy\"; The Golem is \"about as inoffensive a social analysis of science as one could wish for\"; they cite historical work on the slow reception of relativity. Mermin replied on the same page that \"the rightness/wrongness axis is not a relevant dimension in their kind of sociological analysis of science\". David Edge, \"The Umpire Strikes Back\" (EASST web text, February 1997; read in a web reproduction): \"A powerful group of American scientists, apparently for political reasons, have chosen to mount a vicious attack on those scholars whom they take to be 'flying from science and reason'. One of their prime targets is 'social constructivism'\"; the Edinburgh group \"has always maintained that reality plays a central role in what comes to be believed about it\". Edge is not on the ssk roster; he is on this debate's roster. Shapin's 1999 defence of methodological relativism is carried by the science → sciencewars edge. Bloor's own reply to the warriors was not found; his 1999 complaint that Latour joined \"the recent contributors to The Flight from Science and Reason\" is on the ssk → latour edge.",
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_collins_pinch_mermin_1996",
    "ss136_edge_1997"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": "sciencewars/golem_physics_today",
   "target_strand_note": "golem_physics_today holds Mermin, Collins and Pinch; Edge sits in sciencewars/higher_superstition.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_03",
   "source": "sciencewars",
   "target": "latour",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Physicists charge Latour with sliding from representations of nature to nature and with misreading relativity (1996–98)",
   "map_label": "Latour attacked: nature and relativity, 1996–98",
   "evidence_note": "In-period texts read. Sokal and Bricmont, Intellectual Impostures (1998; Profile 1999/2003 printing): Latour's works \"contain a great number of propositions formulated so ambiguously that they can hardly be taken literally\" (p. 85); on the Third Rule of Method, \"Note how Latour slips, without comment or argument, from 'Nature's representation' in the first half of this sentence to 'Nature' tout court in the second half\" (p. 85); his 1988 article on relativity \"illustrates perfectly the problems encountered by a sociologist who aims to analyse the content of a scientific theory he does not understand very well\" (p. 115), and \"Latour's analysis is fatally flawed by his inadequate understanding of the theory Einstein is trying to explain\" (p. 121). Sokal (1997/98, cited by note): \"Latour has produced 40 pages of comical misunderstandings of a theory that is nowadays routinely taught to intelligent college freshmen\"; Sokal in Le Monde (31 January 1997, author's translation): Latour \"mixes up ontology and epistemology, and he attacks positions that no one would defend\". Weinberg (New York Review of Books, 1996): \"The objective nature of scientific knowledge has been denied by Andrew Ross and Bruno Latour\". Dissent among physicists: Mermin (Physics Today 50:10, 1997, p. 11) wrote of the relativity essay, \"I believe such attacks miss the point of Latour's essay\". Steve Fuller's report of the Sokal–Latour debate at the LSE (1998) judged that \"Latour won 'on points'\". Latour's replies are the separate latour → sciencewars edge.",
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_sokal_bricmont_1998",
    "ss136_sokal_affair_1998",
    "ss136_sokal_le_monde_1997",
    "ss136_weinberg_1996",
    "ss136_mermin_1997",
    "ss136_fuller_lse_1998"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": null,
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_04",
   "source": "latour",
   "target": "sciencewars",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Latour answers the physicists: science studies \"added reality to science\" and is \"not at war\" (1996–99)",
   "map_label": "Latour replies: \"not at war\", 1996–99",
   "evidence_note": "His own words where read. Pandora's Hope (1999), ch. 1, \"Do You Believe in Reality? News from the Trenches of the Science Wars\" (read in a retyped copy without book pages; to be checked against the printed book): \"in this guerrilla warfare being conducted in the no-man's-land between the 'two cultures,' we were the ones being attacked by militants, activists, sociologists, philosophers, and technophobes of all hues, precisely because of our interest in the inner workings of scientific facts\"; \"If science studies has achieved anything, I thought, surely it has added reality to science, not withdrawn any from it\"; \"we are not at war\"; and, of the other side, \"a few disgruntled cold-war physicists\". Earlier, as quoted by others: Sokal (Le Monde, 31 January 1997) reports Latour telling Libération (3 December 1996) that the hoax was \"a clever joke\" that thrashed people who deserved it but not researchers in science studies; Jardine and Frasca-Spada (SHPS 1997) translate a sentence of Latour's Le Monde article of 18 January 1997 about \"A small number of theoretical physicists deprived of the fat budgets of the cold war\". That article was not read, and the same editors later printed an \"Apology\" (unread), so the wording is given only as theirs. Fuller (1998) reports that at the LSE Latour \"supported\" constructivism but \"opposed\" relativism. Later reading, not evidence for the claim: in 2004 Latour asked, \"Was I wrong to participate in the invention of this field known as science studies?\", while refusing \"to swear that I will never be a constructivist any more\".",
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_latour_pandora_1999",
    "ss136_sokal_le_monde_1997",
    "ss136_jfs_1997",
    "ss136_fuller_lse_1998",
    "ss136_latour_2004"
   ],
