  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_06",
   "source": "sciencewars",
   "target": "sts",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Science studies as a whole charged with relativism and hostility to science (1994–98)",
   "map_label": "Science studies charged with relativism, 1994–98",
   "evidence_note": "In-period texts. Jardine and Frasca-Spada (SHPS 1997, §I, read) report that Gross and Levitt went after \"exponents of science studies -- that is, historians, philosophers and sociologists of science\" (the book itself unread). Sokal (1997/98, read) disclaims that the hoax proves \"the whole field of cultural studies, or cultural studies of science -- much less sociology of science -- is nonsense\", yet attacks \"the sloppy thinking and glib relativism that have become prevalent in many parts of Science Studies\". The Social Text editors (Robbins and Ross, 1996, read) say science studies and cultural studies had \"been subject recently to so much derision from conservatives in science\". Koertge's anthology (1998; publisher abstract, not her text) addresses the view in \"Science, Technology and Society Studies (STS)\" that scientific results are \"social constructions\". An STS reply in its own words was not read: the Social Text essays (Harding, Fuller, Winner and others) are unread, and Robbins and Ross wrote as cultural-studies editors, so no sts → sciencewars edge is drawn.",
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_jfs_1997",
    "ss136_sokal_affair_1998",
    "ss136_robbins_ross_1996",
    "ss136_koertge_1998",
    "ss136_gross_levitt_1994"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": null,
   "target_strand_note": "No sts strand fits a field-wide charge; the laboratory and situated strands are only parts of what was attacked.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_07",
   "source": "sciencewars",
   "target": "kuhn",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Physicists blame an \"immoderate\" Kuhn for founding relativism about science, while sparing the \"moderate\" Kuhn and leaving him to historians (1996–98)",
   "map_label": "The \"immoderate Kuhn\" blamed, 1996–98",
   "evidence_note": "In-period texts read. Sokal and Bricmont (1998; Profile 1999/2003 printing), following Tim Maudlin, separate \"a moderate Kuhn and his immoderate brother\": \"We have no objection of principle to the moderate Kuhn, and we leave to historians the task of investigating the extent to which these ideas are correct in concrete situations. By contrast, the immoderate Kuhn - who became, perhaps involuntarily, one of the founding fathers of contemporary relativism - thinks that changes of paradigm are due principally to non-empirical factors\" (p. 70). Weinberg (1996) lists Kuhn, \"as I understand them\", among those who deny \"The objective nature of scientific knowledge\"; in 1998 he wrote that Kuhn's later conclusions \"have made Kuhn a hero to the philosophers, historians, sociologists, and cultural critics who question the objective character of scientific knowledge\", and that \"Kuhn made the shift from one paradigm to another seem more like a religious conversion than an exercise of reason.\" Sokal's parody (1996, n. 2) cites Kuhn (1970) first among the \"revisionist studies\" it mocks. This is a critique of Kuhn and of his reception; Kuhn died in 1996 and did not reply. His own earlier rejection of irrationalism (\"labels which I categorically reject\", 1970) is recorded on the science → kuhn edge.",
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_sokal_bricmont_1998",
    "ss136_weinberg_1996",
    "ss136_weinberg_1998",
    "ss136_sokal_transgressing_1996"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": null,
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_08",
   "source": "sciencewars",
   "target": "language",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "The hoax and Intellectual Impostures attack postmodernist literary theory and French theorists' uses of science (1996–98)",
   "map_label": "Postmodern theory attacked, 1996–98",
   "evidence_note": "In-period texts read. Sokal's confession (Lingua Franca, 1996) targets \"the intellectual arrogance of Theory --- meaning postmodernist literary theory\" and argues that \"The recent turn of many 'progressive' or 'leftist' academic humanists and social scientists toward one or another form of epistemic relativism betrays this worthy heritage\". His parody (1996) presents \"Irigaray's and Hayles' exegeses\" among its exhibits; in Le Monde (1997) he writes that among the Masters \"one indeed finds Derrida and Lacan, Aronowitz and Haraway\". Sokal and Bricmont's book (1997; English 1998) is built around chapters on Lacan, Kristeva, Irigaray, Baudrillard, Deleuze and Guattari and Virilio; those chapters were not read here, and the read chapters (pp. 49–96, 115–123) concern epistemic relativism in the philosophy and sociology of science. Richard Evans (1999) welcomed Sokal's work on Derrida and Lyotard as done \"far more effectively and knowledgeably than I could ever hope to do\". No reply from the linguistic-turn side was read.",
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_sokal_lf_1996",
    "ss136_sokal_transgressing_1996",
    "ss136_sokal_le_monde_1997",
    "ss136_sokal_bricmont_1998",
    "ss136_evans_response_1999"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": null,
   "target_strand_note": "The book's targets range across several language strands (deconstruction, signifier_subject and others); none is chosen.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_09",
   "source": "truthdebate",
   "target": "ssk",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Appleby, Hunt and Jacob judge the strong programme's relativism inadequate to historical and scientific truth (1994)",
   "map_label": "Telling the Truth against the strong programme, 1994",
   "evidence_note": "The historians' own words: Telling the Truth about History (1994), read in the Norton 2011 e-book, which has no print page numbers; cited by chapter. Ch. 5 (\"Discovering the Clay Feet of Science\"): the social constructionists \"could not explain the way in which technical problems, or new natural phenomena like viruses, set whole research agendas\"; the social insight \"does not, however, underwrite the more extreme claims of strong programmers who have never offered an adequate theory to address the complexity of circumstances and situations that produce reasonably true human knowledge\"; \"relativism fails the needs of historians just as it skirts the possible existence of truths in science\" (n. 24 cites Barnes and Bloor, \"Relativism, Rationalism and the Sociology of Knowledge\"). Ch. 7 calls social constructionists \"once called strong programmers\". The book predates Higher Superstition and the hoax. No SSK reply to it was found; Shapin's 1999 complaint that methodological relativism is \"routinely misrepresented\" (n. 6) is a general answer, not a reply to this book. Print pages remain to be checked.",
   "source_ids": [
    "appleby_hunt_jacob",
    "ss136_shapin_1999"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": "ssk/strong_programme",
   "target_strand_note": "The book names Barnes and Bloor.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_10",
   "source": "truthdebate",
   "target": "latour",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Telling the Truth about History assimilates Latour's laboratory studies to postmodernism (1994); Jacob carries the objection to the Science Wars anthology (1998)",
   "map_label": "Latour as postmodernist, 1994–98",
   "evidence_note": "Telling the Truth about History (1994; Norton 2011 e-book, no print pages), ch. 6 (\"Postmodernism and the Crisis of Modernity\"), quotes the claim that \"'Nothing special'\" and \"'nothing extraordinary'\" happens in the laboratory, attributes it in n. 9 to Latour, \"Give Me a Laboratory and I Will Raise the World\" (1983), and comments: \"Latour calls himself an 'a-modernist,' but his position seems indistinguishable from postmodernism.\" Margaret Jacob, one of the three authors, then wrote \"Reflections on Bruno Latour's Version of the Seventeenth Century\" for A House Built on Sand (1998). Only the publisher's abstract was read; it says the essay \"seeks to show how wrong-headed\" We Have Never Been Modern is about early modern politics. That wording is the publisher's, not Jacob's. Moderate support: one sentence of the book, plus an abstract. No reply by Latour to these historians was found.",
   "source_ids": [
    "appleby_hunt_jacob",
    "ss136_koertge_1998"
   ],
