   "evidence_note": "In-period texts read. Sokal, \"What the Social Text Affair Does and Does Not Prove\" (author's text, 1997; printed 1998; cited by note): \"The extreme versions of social constructivism and relativism -- such as the Edinburgh 'strong programme' -- are, I think, largely based on the same failure to distinguish clearly between ontology, epistemology, and the sociology of knowledge\"; he quotes Collins (n. 10) as saying \"the natural world has a small or non-existent role in the construction of scientific knowledge\", while allowing that Collins may intend a methodological injunction. Sokal and Bricmont, Intellectual Impostures (1998; Profile 1999/2003 printing): most scientists \"point out the substantial missing piece in this kind of explanation: Nature itself\" (p. 79); the strong programme \"is ambiguous in its intent; and, depending on how one resolves the ambiguity, it becomes either a valid and mildly interesting corrective to the most naive psychological and sociological notions - reminding us that 'true beliefs have causes too' - or else a gross and blatant error\" (p. 84). Mermin, Physics Today 49:3 (1996), p. 11: Collins and Pinch \"infer from these studies a seriously deficient picture of the scientific enterprise\"; The Golem is \"an instructive demonstration of what can go wrong if you focus too strongly on the social perspective\". Mermin frames this as a question of balance, not a refutation. Gross and Levitt (1994; book unread) are known here only through Edge (1997, n. 4), who reports that they name \"the 'strong programme' in sociology of science associated with the Edinburgh school\" (p. 25) and Bloor (p. 52) while listing no work by Barnes or Bloor in their references. The replies are the separate ssk → sciencewars edge.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "Strong programme and The Golem attacked, 1994–98",
   "target_strand": "ssk/strong_programme",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_01"
  },
  {
   "source": "ssk",
   "target": "sciencewars",
   "relationship": "Sociologists of scientific knowledge answer that their methodological relativism was misread as hostility to science (1996–97)",
   "type": "critique",
