   ],
   "evidence_note": "In-period replies in the sociologists' own words. Collins and Pinch, letter in Physics Today 49:7 (1996), p. 11, answering Mermin: \"Many scientists, especially those who have become spokespersons, are evidently scientific fundamentalists. ... Where we sociologists think of ourselves as trying to gain social and historical insight into science, the fundamentalists see heresy\"; The Golem is \"about as inoffensive a social analysis of science as one could wish for\"; they cite historical work on the slow reception of relativity. Mermin replied on the same page that \"the rightness/wrongness axis is not a relevant dimension in their kind of sociological analysis of science\". David Edge, \"The Umpire Strikes Back\" (EASST web text, February 1997; read in a web reproduction): \"A powerful group of American scientists, apparently for political reasons, have chosen to mount a vicious attack on those scholars whom they take to be 'flying from science and reason'. One of their prime targets is 'social constructivism'\"; the Edinburgh group \"has always maintained that reality plays a central role in what comes to be believed about it\". Edge is not on the ssk roster; he is on this debate's roster. Shapin's 1999 defence of methodological relativism is carried by the science → sciencewars edge. Bloor's own reply to the warriors was not found; his 1999 complaint that Latour joined \"the recent contributors to The Flight from Science and Reason\" is on the ssk → latour edge.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "SSK replies: \"scientific fundamentalists\", 1996–97",
   "target_strand": "sciencewars/golem_physics_today",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_02"
  },
  {
   "source": "sciencewars",
