   "evidence_note": "His own words where read. Pandora's Hope (1999), ch. 1, \"Do You Believe in Reality? News from the Trenches of the Science Wars\" (read in a retyped copy without book pages; to be checked against the printed book): \"in this guerrilla warfare being conducted in the no-man's-land between the 'two cultures,' we were the ones being attacked by militants, activists, sociologists, philosophers, and technophobes of all hues, precisely because of our interest in the inner workings of scientific facts\"; \"If science studies has achieved anything, I thought, surely it has added reality to science, not withdrawn any from it\"; \"we are not at war\"; and, of the other side, \"a few disgruntled cold-war physicists\". Earlier, as quoted by others: Sokal (Le Monde, 31 January 1997) reports Latour telling Libération (3 December 1996) that the hoax was \"a clever joke\" that thrashed people who deserved it but not researchers in science studies; Jardine and Frasca-Spada (SHPS 1997) translate a sentence of Latour's Le Monde article of 18 January 1997 about \"A small number of theoretical physicists deprived of the fat budgets of the cold war\". That article was not read, and the same editors later printed an \"Apology\" (unread), so the wording is given only as theirs. Fuller (1998) reports that at the LSE Latour \"supported\" constructivism but \"opposed\" relativism. Later reading, not evidence for the claim: in 2004 Latour asked, \"Was I wrong to participate in the invention of this field known as science studies?\", while refusing \"to swear that I will never be a constructivist any more\".",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "Latour replies: \"not at war\", 1996–99",
   "target_strand": "sciencewars/impostures_latour",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_04"
