   "evidence_note": "In-period texts. Jardine and Frasca-Spada (SHPS 1997, §I, read) report that Gross and Levitt went after \"exponents of science studies -- that is, historians, philosophers and sociologists of science\" (the book itself unread). Sokal (1997/98, read) disclaims that the hoax proves \"the whole field of cultural studies, or cultural studies of science -- much less sociology of science -- is nonsense\", yet attacks \"the sloppy thinking and glib relativism that have become prevalent in many parts of Science Studies\". The Social Text editors (Robbins and Ross, 1996, read) say science studies and cultural studies had \"been subject recently to so much derision from conservatives in science\". Koertge's anthology (1998; publisher abstract, not her text) addresses the view in \"Science, Technology and Society Studies (STS)\" that scientific results are \"social constructions\". An STS reply in its own words was not read: the Social Text essays (Harding, Fuller, Winner and others) are unread, and Robbins and Ross wrote as cultural-studies editors, so no sts → sciencewars edge is drawn.",
