  },
  {
   "source": "truthdebate",
   "target": "ssk",
   "relationship": "Appleby, Hunt and Jacob judge the strong programme's relativism inadequate to historical and scientific truth (1994)",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "source_ids": [
    "appleby_hunt_jacob",
    "ss136_shapin_1999"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "The historians' own words: Telling the Truth about History (1994), read in the Norton 2011 e-book, which has no print page numbers; cited by chapter. Ch. 5 (\"Discovering the Clay Feet of Science\"): the social constructionists \"could not explain the way in which technical problems, or new natural phenomena like viruses, set whole research agendas\"; the social insight \"does not, however, underwrite the more extreme claims of strong programmers who have never offered an adequate theory to address the complexity of circumstances and situations that produce reasonably true human knowledge\"; \"relativism fails the needs of historians just as it skirts the possible existence of truths in science\" (n. 24 cites Barnes and Bloor, \"Relativism, Rationalism and the Sociology of Knowledge\"). Ch. 7 calls social constructionists \"once called strong programmers\". The book predates Higher Superstition and the hoax. No SSK reply to it was found; Shapin's 1999 complaint that methodological relativism is \"routinely misrepresented\" (n. 6) is a general answer, not a reply to this book. Print pages remain to be checked.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "Telling the Truth against the strong programme, 1994",
   "target_strand": "ssk/strong_programme",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_09"
  },
  {
   "source": "truthdebate",
   "target": "latour",
   "relationship": "Telling the Truth about History assimilates Latour's laboratory studies to postmodernism (1994); Jacob carries the objection to the Science Wars anthology (1998)",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "source_ids": [
    "appleby_hunt_jacob",
    "ss136_koertge_1998"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "Telling the Truth about History (1994; Norton 2011 e-book, no print pages), ch. 6 (\"Postmodernism and the Crisis of Modernity\"), quotes the claim that \"'Nothing special'\" and \"'nothing extraordinary'\" happens in the laboratory, attributes it in n. 9 to Latour, \"Give Me a Laboratory and I Will Raise the World\" (1983), and comments: \"Latour calls himself an 'a-modernist,' but his position seems indistinguishable from postmodernism.\" Margaret Jacob, one of the three authors, then wrote \"Reflections on Bruno Latour's Version of the Seventeenth Century\" for A House Built on Sand (1998). Only the publisher's abstract was read; it says the essay \"seeks to show how wrong-headed\" We Have Never Been Modern is about early modern politics. That wording is the publisher's, not Jacob's. Moderate support: one sentence of the book, plus an abstract. No reply by Latour to these historians was found.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "Latour as postmodernist, 1994–98",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_10"
  },
  {
   "source": "sciencewars",
   "target": "truthdebate",
   "relationship": "Two 1990s disputes over relativism and truth: historians defending historical knowledge, and scientists attacking science studies",
   "type": "connection",
   "relationship_kind": "comparison",
   "directed": false,
   "source_ids": [
    "appleby_hunt_jacob",
    "ss136_evans_response_1999",
    "ss136_koertge_1998"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "Comparison only. The disputes overlap in time and in people but neither is a critique of the other. Margaret Jacob took part in both: Telling the Truth about History (1994) already criticised the strong programme and Latour from the historians' side, and she wrote for A House Built on Sand (1998; abstract only). Richard Evans, a truthdebate historian, cited Sokal approvingly in 1999 as having done \"far more effectively and knowledgeably\" what he had only touched on in criticising Derrida and Lyotard. The historians' book predates the Science Wars, and Evans endorses Sokal rather than criticising him, so no critique edge is drawn in either direction. The Science Wars concerned the natural sciences and their students; the truth debate concerned historical writing.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "Truth, relativism and two 1990s disputes",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_sw_rel_11"
  },
  {
   "source": "science",
   "target": "kuhn",
   "relationship": "Popper, Lakatos and the historian L. Pearce Williams criticise Kuhn's normal science and theory change: indoctrination, irrationalism, \"mob psychology\" and too little history (1965–70), answered by Kuhn",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "source_ids": [
    "popper_normal_debate",
    "ss136_cgk_1970",
    "ss136_kuhn_postscript_1970"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "In-period texts read in Lakatos and Musgrave, eds., Criticism and the Growth of Knowledge (1970; the 1965 Bedford College symposium). Popper, already a critic on the science roster: the normal scientist \"has been taught in a dogmatic spirit: he is a victim of indoctrination\" (p. 53); \"Kuhn's logic is the logic of historical relativism\" (p. 55); the Myth of the Framework \"is, in our time, the central bulwark of irrationalism\" (p. 56); yet \"'Normal' science, in Kuhn's sense, exists\" (p. 52). Lakatos, a contributor on the science roster: Kuhn's message \"had some authoritarian and irrationalist overtones\" (p. 92 n. 3); \"in Kuhn's view scientific revolution is irrational, a matter for mob psychology\" (p. 178, italicised in the original); he also absorbs Kuhn, offering his research programmes as a reconstruction of Kuhn's \"socio-psychological concept of 'paradigm'\" (p. 179 n. 1). L. Pearce Williams, a historian of science, aims at Kuhn and Popper alike: \"the history of science cannot bear such a load at this time. We simply do not know enough to permit a philosophical structure to be erected on a historical foundation\"; \"As a historian, then, I must view both Popper and Kuhn with a somewhat jaundiced eye\" (p. 50), while continuing to use both as guides. Kuhn's replies: charges of \"irrationality, relativism, and the defence of mob rule\" are \"labels which I categorically reject\" (p. 234); such misinterpretations \"are voiced only by philosophers\" (p. 260); to Pearce Williams, \"There is no area in which I am more deeply conscious of the need for additional historical research, though I dissent from the conclusions Pearce Williams draws from that need\" (pp. 251–252); and in the 1970 Postscript, good reasons \"function as values\" (p. 199) and \"That is not a relativist's position\" (p. 206). The irrationalism charge came from philosophers on the science roster; the historian's objection was evidential. edge_332 (science → kuhn, influence) stays.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "Normal science and irrationalism disputed, 1965–70",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_cr_rel_01"
  },
  {
   "source": "latour",
   "target": "ssk",
   "relationship": "Latour and Callon: the strong programme's symmetry is itself asymmetrical, explaining nature by society, so SSK must take \"one more turn\" (1992–99), after an acknowledged debt",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_latour_one_more_turn_1992",
    "ss136_callon_latour_1992",
    "ss136_latour_never_modern_1993",
    "latour_bloor_reply_primary",
    "latour_pasteur",
    "bloor_anti_latour_primary"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "Latour's own words, read. Debt first: in 1988 he wrote that Bloor \"has most clearly defined the task of any sociology of science by introducing the notion of symmetry\" and that \"the principle of symmetry remains the basis of most work in the area\", while dropping social explanation (Pasteurization of France, p. 256 n. 21); in 1999, \"I feel entirely in David's debt\" (p. 114); edge_685 records that debt. Critique: \"One More Turn after the Social Turn\" (1992; author's typescript, §2): \"Bloor's book (1976) was the high tide mark of this asymmetrical philosophy\"; \"Society was supposed to explain Nature!\" (p. 278 as cited by Bloor); §3: the \"intermediary solution —social realism alternating with natural relativism— advocated for many years by colleagues like David Bloor or Harry Collins\" is \"in the long run, counterproductive for the field as well as for their own program\". Callon and Latour (1992): \"We have never been interested in giving a social explanation of anything, but we want to explain society\" (p. 348); their yardstick lets them \"read most of SSK as 'reactionary,' because they start from a closed definition of the social and then use this repertoire as an explanation of nature\" (p. 349); Collins \"simply shifts the burden to the Edinburgh school\" (p. 355), though \"We learned a great deal from Collins's work\" (p. 344). We Have Never Been Modern (1993, p. 94; OCR gaps): Bloor's principle of symmetry \"leads rapidly to an impasse\"; \"Constructivist where Nature is concerned, it is realistic about [Society]\". \"For David Bloor… and Beyond\" (1999): \"The Strong Program was useful and still is against the few remaining epistemologists. It has become an obstacle for the continuation of science studies\" (p. 116). Bloor's reply: Latour's \"criticisms are based on a systematic misrepresentation of the position he rejects\" (Anti-Latour, 1999, p. 82); see ssk → latour. Callon has no entry and sits on the sts roster.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "The strong programme as an obstacle, 1992–99",
   "target_strand": "ssk/symmetry_dispute",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_cr_rel_02"
  },
  {
   "source": "ssk",
   "target": "latour",
   "relationship": "Bloor answers that Latour misrepresents the Strong Program and that his own alternative is \"obscurantism\" (1999); Collins and Yearley had called the actor-network approach a \"backward step\" (1992)",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "source_ids": [
    "bloor_anti_latour_primary",
    "latour_bloor_reply_primary",
    "ss136_callon_latour_1992"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "Bloor's own words, read in \"Anti-Latour\" (SHPS 30, 1999): \"Bruno Latour is a vehement critic of the sociology of knowledge in general, and the Strong Program in particular\" (p. 81); \"I think Latour is wrong ... If anything they are a step backwards\" (p. 82); \"his criticisms are based on a systematic misrepresentation of the position he rejects, and ... his own approach, in so far as it is different, is unworkable\" (p. 82); the charge that SSK explains nature by society \"is a profound misrepresentation. The aim isn't to explain nature, but to explain shared beliefs about nature\" (p. 87); Latour's vocabulary of actants \"looks like a formula for imposing confusion on ourselves: it is obscurantism raised to the level of a general methodological principle\" (p. 97); \"Latour has endorsed, and encouraged, a wholly false stereotype of the Strong Program\", joining \"the recent contributors to The Flight from Science and Reason\" (pp. 110–111). He partly concedes: \"Latour, rightly, rejects the symmetry principle understood in this way\" (p. 84). He cites other critiques he finds \"devastating\" (p. 95), including Collins and Yearley (1992), Schaffer (1991) and Shapin (1988), none read here. Collins and Yearley's chapter is known only through Callon and Latour's quotations (1992, p. 347): \"The consequences of the semiotic method [of Latour] amount to a backward step\". Latour's reply acknowledges the charge: \"The result is a devastating critique\" (1999, p. 113) and calls Schaffer's similar critique \"exactly as ill-conceived\" (n. 6). Bloor's rejoinder (SHPS 30, pp. 131–136) was not located.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "Bloor's \"Anti-Latour\", 1999",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_cr_rel_03"
  },
  {
   "source": "kuhn",
   "target": "ssk",
   "relationship": "Kuhn calls the strong programme's claims \"absurd: an example of deconstruction gone mad\" (1991)",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_kuhn_road_2000"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "Kuhn's own words, read: \"The Trouble with the Historical Philosophy of Science\" (Rothschild Lecture, Harvard, 19 November 1991; published 1992; in The Road Since Structure, 2000): \"I am among those who have found the claims of the strong program absurd: an example of deconstruction gone mad. And the more qualified sociological and historical formulations that currently strive to replace it are, in my view, scarcely more satisfactory\" (p. 110); the strong program \"has been widely understood as claiming that power and interest are all there are\" (p. 110). In the 1995 Athens interview (same volume) he says of Shapin and Schaffer's Leviathan and the Air-Pump, \"The scholarship is often damn good!\", while objecting that they \"totally miss\" the barometer's \"vastly greater explanatory power\" (pp. 316–317); that remark concerns the science roster's practice strand and is context only. No SSK reply to the lecture was read. The SSK reading of Kuhn ran the other way, as appropriation (Barnes, T. S. Kuhn and Social Science, 1982, unread); edge_132 (kuhn → ssk, influence) stays.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "\"Deconstruction gone mad\", 1991",
   "target_strand": "ssk/strong_programme",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_cr_rel_04"
  },
  {
   "source": "consumption",
   "target": "latour",
   "relationship": "A historian of consumption and material culture criticises actor-network theory's \"flat view of modern history\" while proposing to historicise its concerns (2009)",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "source_ids": [
    "ss136_trentmann_2009"
   ],
   "evidence_note": "Dated 2009. Frank Trentmann, \"Materiality in the Future of History\" (Journal of British Studies 48, 2009; read in full). He writes as a historian of consumption (author note: director of the Cultures of Consumption research programme, 2002–07) and sets material-culture studies against material politics (p. 285). \"The most influential (if controversial) platform for exploring the 'agency' of things in the past couple of decades has been actor-network theory (ANT). Spearheaded by Bruno Latour, Michel Callon, John Law, and others\" (p. 290); n. 33 notes \"the difficulty of integrating historical formations of power and culture in an analysis primarily concerned with reconstructing a network of actors and actants at a specific moment in time\" (p. 290). \"It is perhaps ironic that a theory like ANT, so concerned with resisting grand narratives and abstractions (like 'society'), draws on such a flat view of modern history\"; \"It is hard to see a general turning away from things, as presumed by Latour and others\" (p. 291); \"this rather monochromatic view of modernity supposedly so divorced from things\" (p. 301). The critique is qualified: \"Before simply rejecting Latour as problematic history, however, we might historicize his concerns into questions for research\" (p. 301), and whatever one thinks of the claim that things have agency, \"things and humans are inseparably interwoven in mutually constitutive relationships\" (p. 307). No reply by Latour was found. A historian's critique of Latour; not a claim that consumption history as a field rejected ANT.",
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "map_label": "ANT's flat modernity questioned, 2009",
   "proposal_id": "ss136_cr_rel_05"
  }
 ],
