## 10. Dated later readings (2001–2005), labelled as such

- Latour, "Why Has Critique Run out of Steam? From Matters of Fact to Matters of Concern", *Critical Inquiry* 30 (2004) (author's PDF, read; cited without pages): "dangerous extremists are using the very same argument of social construction to destroy hard-won evidence that could save our lives. Was I wrong to participate in the invention of this field known as science studies? Is it enough to say that we did not really mean what we said?"; "I am not trying to reverse course, to become reactionary, to regret what I have done, to swear that I will never be a constructivist any more." A later reading that the atlas could cite as Latour's own retrospective on the Science Wars.
- Labinger and Collins (eds), *The One Culture? A Conversation about Science* (2001); Ashman and Barringer (eds), *After the Science Wars* (2001; cited by 2 Canadian History PhDs); Golinski, *Making Natural Knowledge*, 2nd ed. with new preface (Chicago 2005). Not read.
