    "relationship": "Popper, Lakatos and the historian L. Pearce Williams criticise Kuhn's normal science and theory change: indoctrination, irrationalism, \"mob psychology\" and too little history (1965–70), answered by Kuhn",
    "map_label": "Normal science and irrationalism disputed, 1965–70",
    "evidence_note": "In-period texts read in Lakatos and Musgrave, eds., Criticism and the Growth of Knowledge (1970; the 1965 Bedford College symposium). Popper, already a critic on the science roster: the normal scientist \"has been taught in a dogmatic spirit: he is a victim of indoctrination\" (p. 53); \"Kuhn's logic is the logic of historical relativism\" (p. 55); the Myth of the Framework \"is, in our time, the central bulwark of irrationalism\" (p. 56); yet \"'Normal' science, in Kuhn's sense, exists\" (p. 52). Lakatos, a contributor on the science roster: Kuhn's message \"had some authoritarian and irrationalist overtones\" (p. 92 n. 3); \"in Kuhn's view scientific revolution is irrational, a matter for mob psychology\" (p. 178, italicised in the original); he also absorbs Kuhn, offering his research programmes as a reconstruction of Kuhn's \"socio-psychological concept of 'paradigm'\" (p. 179 n. 1). L. Pearce Williams, a historian of science, aims at Kuhn and Popper alike: \"the history of science cannot bear such a load at this time. We simply do not know enough to permit a philosophical structure to be erected on a historical foundation\"; \"As a historian, then, I must view both Popper and Kuhn with a somewhat jaundiced eye\" (p. 50), while continuing to use both as guides. Kuhn's replies: charges of \"irrationality, relativism, and the defence of mob rule\" are \"labels which I categorically reject\" (p. 234); such misinterpretations \"are voiced only by philosophers\" (p. 260); to Pearce Williams, \"There is no area in which I am more deeply conscious of the need for additional historical research, though I dissent from the conclusions Pearce Williams draws from that need\" (pp. 251–252); and in the 1970 Postscript, good reasons \"function as values\" (p. 199) and \"That is not a relativist's position\" (p. 206). The irrationalism charge came from philosophers on the science roster; the historian's objection was evidential. edge_332 (science → kuhn, influence) stays.",
