    "directed": true,
    "relationship": "Latour and Callon: the strong programme's symmetry is itself asymmetrical, explaining nature by society, so SSK must take \"one more turn\" (1992–99), after an acknowledged debt",
    "map_label": "The strong programme as an obstacle, 1992–99",
    "evidence_note": "Latour's own words, read. Debt first: in 1988 he wrote that Bloor \"has most clearly defined the task of any sociology of science by introducing the notion of symmetry\" and that \"the principle of symmetry remains the basis of most work in the area\", while dropping social explanation (Pasteurization of France, p. 256 n. 21); in 1999, \"I feel entirely in David's debt\" (p. 114); edge_685 records that debt. Critique: \"One More Turn after the Social Turn\" (1992; author's typescript, §2): \"Bloor's book (1976) was the high tide mark of this asymmetrical philosophy\"; \"Society was supposed to explain Nature!\" (p. 278 as cited by Bloor); §3: the \"intermediary solution —social realism alternating with natural relativism— advocated for many years by colleagues like David Bloor or Harry Collins\" is \"in the long run, counterproductive for the field as well as for their own program\". Callon and Latour (1992): \"We have never been interested in giving a social explanation of anything, but we want to explain society\" (p. 348); their yardstick lets them \"read most of SSK as 'reactionary,' because they start from a closed definition of the social and then use this repertoire as an explanation of nature\" (p. 349); Collins \"simply shifts the burden to the Edinburgh school\" (p. 355), though \"We learned a great deal from Collins's work\" (p. 344). We Have Never Been Modern (1993, p. 94; OCR gaps): Bloor's principle of symmetry \"leads rapidly to an impasse\"; \"Constructivist where Nature is concerned, it is realistic about [Society]\". \"For David Bloor… and Beyond\" (1999): \"The Strong Program was useful and still is against the few remaining epistemologists. It has become an obstacle for the continuation of science studies\" (p. 116). Bloor's reply: Latour's \"criticisms are based on a systematic misrepresentation of the position he rejects\" (Anti-Latour, 1999, p. 82); see ssk → latour. Callon has no entry and sits on the sts roster.",
