    "relationship": "A historian of consumption and material culture criticises actor-network theory's \"flat view of modern history\" while proposing to historicise its concerns (2009)",
    "map_label": "ANT's flat modernity questioned, 2009",
    "evidence_note": "Dated 2009. Frank Trentmann, \"Materiality in the Future of History\" (Journal of British Studies 48, 2009; read in full). He writes as a historian of consumption (author note: director of the Cultures of Consumption research programme, 2002–07) and sets material-culture studies against material politics (p. 285). \"The most influential (if controversial) platform for exploring the 'agency' of things in the past couple of decades has been actor-network theory (ANT). Spearheaded by Bruno Latour, Michel Callon, John Law, and others\" (p. 290); n. 33 notes \"the difficulty of integrating historical formations of power and culture in an analysis primarily concerned with reconstructing a network of actors and actants at a specific moment in time\" (p. 290). \"It is perhaps ironic that a theory like ANT, so concerned with resisting grand narratives and abstractions (like 'society'), draws on such a flat view of modern history\"; \"It is hard to see a general turning away from things, as presumed by Latour and others\" (p. 291); \"this rather monochromatic view of modernity supposedly so divorced from things\" (p. 301). The critique is qualified: \"Before simply rejecting Latour as problematic history, however, we might historicize his concerns into questions for research\" (p. 301), and whatever one thinks of the claim that things have agency, \"things and humans are inseparably interwoven in mutually constitutive relationships\" (p. 307). No reply by Latour was found. A historian's critique of Latour; not a claim that consumption history as a field rejected ANT.",
