NODE science History of science Studies scientific knowledge, practices, instruments, institutions, and their historical settings. Histories of ideas, social and cultural histories, and material approaches coexist. The field predates Kuhn and STS, which transform parts of its questions without founding or exhausting it. Kuhn is a central reorientation, not the field’s sole founder. Sarton, Koyré, Merton, and historians of experimental practice distinguish important approaches; Yates and Foucault are additional contributions. Chinese science and women’s scientific work expand this selection; it still needs fuller coverage of other regions and material practices. Named Chinese-series contributors and Daston/Galison’s image and instrument studies deepen the selection. Publication credits do not replace primary argument checks; wider regional coverage remains selective. Arabic mathematics, colonial Indian science, African healing and a Beninese knowledge programme are separate selected projects, not regional proxies or exhaustive coverage. {'reviewed_through': 2000, 'basis': 'Reviewed through 2000 in the original 1920–2000 experiment. Later work is not yet surveyed for this entry; selected later interventions, where present, are dated individually.'}
STRANDS [('ideas', 'Historical concepts and earlier disciplinary projects'), ('kuhn', 'Scientific communities and revolutions'), ('social', 'Scientific institutions and social contexts'), ('practice', 'Experiment, credibility, and knowledge-making'), ('chinese_science', 'Chinese science and comparative questions'), ('women_environment', 'Women, scientific careers, and environments'), ('mnemonics_occult_method', 'Mnemonic traditions, occult inquiry and scientific method'), ('human_sciences_professions', 'Human sciences, classifications and professions'), ('objectivity_images', 'Images and historical forms of objectivity'), ('instruments_coordination', 'Instruments and coordination'), ('arabic_mathematics', 'Arabic mathematics and conceptual history'), ('colonial_indian_science', 'Colonial Indian science'), ('endogenous_knowledge', 'Endogenous knowledge and research priorities'), ('african_health_healing', 'African health and healing histories'), ('normal_science_debates', 'Normal science, criticism and research programmes'), ('bacon_metaphor_dispute', 'Bacon’s metaphors and experimental power')]

NODE ssk Sociology of scientific knowledge / SSK Investigates how scientific beliefs become accepted within social practices and institutions. The Edinburgh Strong Programme seeks causal explanations of both accepted and rejected knowledge using the same kinds of explanation. SSK includes different programmes; it is neither all sociology of science nor all STS. Collins’s replication studies and Pinch’s work on controversies expose additional empirical programmes; these differ from treating SSK as only Bloor’s principles. Bloor explicitly includes material causes and rejects equating symmetry with equal credibility. His disagreement with Latour concerns the analytical roles of objects and society. The strong programme and related sociology concern explanations of knowledge; they are not identical to all STS or laboratory ethnography. {'reviewed_through': 2000, 'basis': 'Reviewed through 2000 in the original 1920–2000 experiment. Later work is not yet surveyed for this entry; selected later interventions, where present, are dated individually.'}
STRANDS [('strong_programme', 'The Strong Programme and collective knowledge'), ('replication', 'Replication, tacit skill, and controversy'), ('symmetry_dispute', 'Symmetry, objects and the Bloor–Latour dispute')]

NODE sts Science and technology studies / STS An interdisciplinary field examining how knowledge, technologies, institutions, and social arrangements develop together. Historical, sociological, ethnographic, and policy-oriented inquiries coexist. SSK and laboratory studies contribute to STS but do not define its entire scope. Knorr Cetina’s comparative laboratory cultures, Harding’s feminist epistemology, and Sussex/Cornell institution building add distinct approaches and histories. Sociological, ethnographic, technological, and policy-oriented projects interact. A discipline-level label cannot substitute for a named research programme. {'reviewed_through': 2000, 'basis': 'Reviewed through 2000 in the original 1920–2000 experiment. Later work is not yet surveyed for this entry; selected later interventions, where present, are dated individually.'}
STRANDS [('laboratory', 'Laboratory ethnography'), ('technological_systems', 'Systems and the social construction of technology'), ('regulation', 'Expertise and public decisions'), ('situated', 'Feminist questions of objectivity'), ('epistemic_cultures', 'Different cultures of knowing'), ('standpoint_plurality', 'Standpoint and differentiated knowers'), ('policy_institutions', 'Policy research and institution building'), ('translation_association', 'Translation, association and collective publication')]

NODE consumption Consumption & material culture Examines commercial promotion, household demand, goods’ changing value, taste, gender and provisioning. McKendrick, Brewer and Plumb distinguish commercial and social changes; de Vries connects consumption with household labor; Mintz links diets to colonial production. Anthropological and feminist approaches ask how objects acquire meanings and how consumer roles depend on unequal social relations. Demand, taste, provisioning, commodities, and material objects are not one theory; gender and empire connect consumption to production. Commodity circulation, cultural biography and goods as communication are distinct questions. De Vries acknowledges conflicting household interests; feminist research does not reduce women to consumers. Much of this selection concerns Europe and the United States; Mintz’s colonial production connects regions but does not make the coverage global in every respect. {'reviewed_through': 2000, 'basis': 'Reviewed through 2000 in the original 1920–2000 experiment. Later work is not yet surveyed for this entry; selected later interventions, where present, are dated individually.'}
STRANDS [('commercialization', 'Commercialization and the consumer-revolution thesis'), ('household_labor', 'Households, labor and demand'), ('commodity_history', 'Commodity histories, labor and empire'), ('circulation_value', 'Circulation and the politics of value'), ('object_biography', 'Cultural biographies and changing commodity status'), ('goods_communication', 'Goods as social communication'), ('taste_position', 'Taste and social position'), ('gender_consumption', 'Gendered consumption, work and identity'), ('inventory_boundaries', 'Inventory evidence and its boundaries'), ('purchasing_work', 'Purchasing as household work'), ('material_meanings', 'Material meanings and unequal possibilities'), ('consumer_chronologies', 'Competing chronologies and questions'), ('ming_consumer_distinctions', 'Ming consumers and social distinctions'), ('elite_sources_limits', 'Whose consumption becomes visible?'), ('relic_careers', 'Relics and changing value'), ('situated_theory_uses', 'Specific historical uses of theory'), ('berg_clifford_luxury', 'Luxury and European consumer culture')]
