# Revision 1.113: expanded journal founding-programme review

Added fourteen sourced founding links, bringing the separate journal graph to 22 founding programmes and one debate. Coverage now includes feminist and gender scholarship, social-science history, economic history, oral history, African source criticism, interdisciplinary quantitative methods, history of science, comparison, environmental history, nationalism, Holocaust studies and historical anthropology. Each edge limits the claim to the programme component supported by its source. Primary editorials, extracts, institutional histories, founder testimony, contemporary reception and abstract-only evidence are explicitly distinguished.

Filled only two previously missing publication starts: Oral History Review 1973 and History in Africa 1974. All existing dates remain unchanged, including Isis’s 1912 foundation distinct from first-volume publication in 1913. Current publication starts: 557, including 545 unchanged catalogued title starts. Fourteen records gain source references and necessary research-journal roles; all identities, bibliography, subject classifications and historical graph content are preserved.

Report: `feedback/journals/founding-expansion-v1.113.md`; a 28-record ledger distinguishes accepted claims, partial research, inherited gap findings and unreviewed leads. Public, medical and urban history remain the strongest dedicated-entry candidates. Broader intellectual history/history of ideas and comparative genocide studies are additional coverage questions requiring research. No new historical field entries are inferred from journal counts.

Snapshot: `drafts/historiography-1920-2000.v1.112.json`. New hash-pinned founding-002 applies after immutable metadata and founding-001. Explicit missing-date checks cannot overwrite existing chronology. 57 Python tests, JavaScript core suite, zero structural errors/four unchanged warnings, exact preservation and offline rebuild checks passed. Six allowlisted assets rebuilt and byte-checked. No deployment or OpenAlex work; journal rendering remains future work.

# Revision 1.112: journal founding programmes and atlas gap audit

Added five founding-programme edges for Past & Present (1952), Technology and Culture (1959), Journal of World History (1990), Geschichte und Gesellschaft (1975) and Journal of Negro History (1916). Retained the existing Annales, History Workshop and Journal of Social History foundations and the P&P narrative exchange. Nine venue edges now comprise eight founding programmes and one debate. Six scoped sources distinguish primary, contemporaneous reception, retrospective testimony and the reused prior GG foreword check. Foundation dates belong to journal events, not the origin of whole fields.

The journal-led audit identifies public history, medical history and urban history as strong candidates for dedicated entries, explicitly documenting their existing partial coverage. Archaeology and regional historiographies are further review priorities. No unresearched historical nodes or generic publication links were added. Report and full subject matrix: `feedback/journals/founding-and-gaps-v1.112.md` / `.json`.

